{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"vinifera-farflung-nyala","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`bindings.json` keeps two indexes that must stay in lock-step: forward\n`bindings[local_id] = {jira_key, …}` and reverse `reverse[jira_key] = local_id`. When a\n`reverse` entry survives the removal of its forward entry, `rebar bridge fsck` reports it\nforever as `store_integrity` / kind `reverse_missing_forward` — and **no supported surface\ncan clean it up**, so the binding-drift canary alerts indefinitely on a benign fault and\nmasks any genuine future integrity problem behind a constant non-zero count.\n\nThis is not hypothetical. It already happened: 13 orphaned reverse keys (REB-410…REB-422)\nsat in the store from at least 2026-08-06 until repaired under\n[rebar:7f69-6d23-ff70-46ff] (nonliteral-spangly-fly), where the repair had to reach past the\npublic API into `BindingStore._data[\"reverse\"]` because nothing else could express it.\n\n## The defect\n\n`BindingStore.unbind()` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py:426-430`):\n\n    def unbind(self, local_id: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Remove binding (for cleanup/rollback).\"\"\"\n        entry = self._data[\"bindings\"].pop(local_id, None)\n        if entry is not None and entry.get(\"jira_key\"):\n            self._data[\"reverse\"].pop(entry[\"jira_key\"], None)\n\nIt unconditionally pops the forward entry but pops the reverse entry **only if the forward\nentry still carries a `jira_key`**. The cleanup of index B is made conditional on the\ncontents of index A, which it has just destroyed. If that condition is ever false while a\nreverse entry exists, the reverse key is orphaned permanently.\n\n**Accuracy note on reachability (measured, not assumed).** In *current* code the guard\nhappens to hold: `reverse` is only ever written where a `jira_key` exists (`:424` in\n`bind_confirm`, `:565` in `note_absent_or_rekey`), and no path clears `jira_key` on a\nconfirmed entry, so `reverse[K] = L` implies `bindings[L].jira_key == K`. So `unbind()` is\n**incidentally** safe today — safe by a whole-file invariant, not by its own logic. It is\none keyless-write or partial-write away from leaking, and it gives a reader no signal that\nits correctness depends on something outside itself.\n\n**The broader and already-realised hole:** only two code paths remove a forward entry —\n`unbind()` (`:428`) and `_retire()` (`:592`, which correctly pops both indexes). **Any other\nroute that removes a forward entry leaks the reverse key permanently**: an out-of-band prune\nor mass-archive that rewrites `bindings.json`, a manual edit, or a merge artifact. The\ntickets branch resolves `bindings.json` as `merge=ours` (`docs/concurrency.md:414`), so a\nconcurrent divergence can drop forward entries wholesale with no reverse pop. That —\nmost plausibly a mid-2026 prune of `ZZ-*TEST` fixture tickets — is what produced the 13\nrepaired keys; the forward entries were removed by a route that was not `unbind()`.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo independent changes; either alone is an improvement, both together close the hole.\n\n1. **Make `unbind()` self-sufficient.** Drop the `jira_key` condition and remove the reverse\n   entry by authority of the reverse index itself, so the method is correct regardless of\n   the forward entry's contents.\n2. **Give the orphan class a cleanup path.** There is currently no verb — `bridge fsck` is\n   audit-only by design, `fsck --repair` covers only the event store, and\n   `tracker-maintenance --clean` covers only stranded index entries and foreign paths. Add a\n   supported repair (a `bridge fsck --repair`-style mode, or a binding-store maintenance\n   verb) that prunes `reverse` keys with no forward entry, guarded so it acts on exactly the\n   audited finding set and refuses on any other integrity kind.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py` — `unbind()` reverse-pop condition.\n- whichever module hosts the new repair verb (`src/rebar/_engine_support/bridge_fsck.py` is\n  audit-only and should stay so; the repair belongs alongside `src/rebar/_commands/`).\n- tests covering both.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Happy path:** `unbind()` on a normally-confirmed binding removes both indexes (unchanged\n  behaviour; guards against regression).\n- **The defect, as a RED test:** construct a store with `reverse[K] = L` where `bindings[L]`\n  exists but has no `jira_key`, call `unbind(L)`, and assert `K` is gone from `reverse`.\n  This fails on today's code and is the test that pins the fix.\n- **Repair verb:** seed a store with a known orphan set, run the verb, assert\n  `store_integrity` goes to 0, the forward map is byte-identical, and `bindings-retired.json`\n  is untouched; assert it refuses/no-ops when a different integrity kind is present.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `unbind()` removes the reverse entry without depending on the forward entry's\n      `jira_key`, with a test that fails before the change and passes after.\n- [ ] A supported verb can prune `reverse_missing_forward` orphans, so this class of drift\n      no longer requires reaching into `BindingStore._data`.\n- [ ] `rebar bridge fsck` still reports `store_integrity` = 0 for a healthy store, and the\n      new verb is a no-op on one.","id":"874a-bda3-ee6f-45eb","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"unbind() leaks reverse bindings when the forward entry lacks a jira_key, and no supported surface can clean them up"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786533049689932001,"uuid":"c27aa596-4cc6-4f6e-8203-900adbbfcf76"}